course: dictionary
lesson: 10486
title: Turkic: a word for languages and peoples
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Turkic is an adjective, so it describes a noun. It has a capital T, like other names of languages and peoples. You will see it in phrases such as «Turkic languages» and «Turkic peoples».
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence: «Arabic spread from Arabia across the Middle East and North Africa, as far as Egypt and Morocco, after the 600s, while Turkic languages spread from Central Asia west to Anatolia.» The words spread from Central Asia west to Anatolia show that Turkic languages travelled over a very large area.
explain: In the second sentence, the clue comes after a comma. «Cossacks were free warrior communities on the Ukrainian steppe, a huge grassy plain.» The words after the comma tell you what a steppe is. Then ask: what word would fit here? Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Turkic is an adjective, and it has a capital T.
fact: Turkic describes a family of related languages, such as Turkish, Azerbaijani, Kazakh, Uzbek, Kyrgyz, Turkmen, Uyghur and Tatar.
fact: «Turkic peoples» means the people who speak these languages and share their cultures.
fact: Turkic peoples live across Central Asia, Turkey and parts of Russia and China.
fact: A steppe is a huge grassy plain, like the one where the Cossacks lived.
exercise: «Turkic languages spread from Central Asia west to Anatolia.» In this sentence, what does Turkic describe? | *Languages | Mountains | Cities | Weather
exercise: Which of these languages is part of the Turkic family? | *Uzbek | English | Welsh | Greek
exercise: «The Cossacks lived on the Ukrainian steppe, a huge grassy plain.» What is a steppe? | A busy city | A deep sea | A small wood | *A huge grassy plain
test: In «Turkic peoples», what does Turkic tell you about the people? | They have a strange kind of weather | *They speak the Turkic languages and share their culture | They live only in cities | They cross rivers every day
test: The Cossack sentence says steppe is «a huge grassy plain». Which clue explains the word steppe? | The word Ukrainian | The word warrior | The word free | *The words after the comma
exam: Which phrase is written correctly? | Turkic Languages | turkic languages | *Turkic languages | TURKIC languages
